A1. Is there a dull kid in your home? Here is something to make it bright. ~ POLISH
A7. A swinging gait. ~ AMBLE
A12. What the new tax on tickets will be. ~ NUISANCE
A14. What father has found he cannot do since son is old enough to have home work. ~ RELAX
A15. To uncork a bottle in a poetic manner. ~ OPE
A16. What one has to do in order to make both ends meet. ~ PLAN
A17. This is not an abbreviation but it is a cute contraction. ~ CRAMP
A18. In order to make this just pass, we will double it. ~ SO
A19. Measures adopted to keep printers busy. ~ ENS
A20. What Congress had to do in order to balance the budget. ~ AGREE
A21. This is bound to go up, but it's not worth hanging on to. ~ TNT
A23. Look out! This is not on the level (abbr.) ~ MT
A24. What a Scotchman would never do if he had to pay the funeral expenses. ~ DEE
A25. Here is Krazy Kat minus her tail. ~ CA
A26. Although this is dumb. it catches on to things very easily. ~ APE
A28. Drunk in America. ~ WATER
A31. The type that gets out of sorts in the morning. ~ PI
A32. This "comes over the mountain" in South America and reminds one more of a Camel than LaPalina. ~ LLAMA
A34. Drunk in France. (plur.) ~ WINES
A35. Drunk in England. ~ ALE
A36. This stepped out of a fairy book in seven league boots. ~ OGRE
A37. Uncle Sam should reduce this if he wishes to retain a good figure. ~ WASTE
A38. This makes a good appearance. ~ MIEN
A39. What one might do if they start out on a shoestring. ~ TIE
A40. A good place to unload. ~ LEVEE
A41. Some, people try to hedge with this. ~ LILAC
A42. Double and redouble and the laugh will be on you. ~ HA
A43. Meet friend Alma. ~ MATER
A44. Although they do not admit it this is the age of some stars. ~ EON
A45. The tail of Mickey Mouse. ~ SE
A46. A Frenchman who is penny wise should know this. ~ SOU
A47. "The top of the morning" to you. (abbr.) ~ AM
A48. A veteran puzzle bird. ~ EMU
A50. This has stirred up many things in China. ~ SPOON
A52. Of course this would get into the wet varnish. ~ LAC
A54. A choice word. ~ OR
A55. They must lead a dog's life in this place. ~ POUND
A56. These are anxiously waiting for the picnic season to open. ~ ANTS
A58. What Gandhi's goat did to show his loyalty and rid India of the British tacks. ~ ATE
A59. More in than out. ~ INNER
A60. These must be in unusual shape in order to hold a square meal with a round steak and a triangle of pie. ~ STOMACHS
A62. Someone will surely make a donkey out of this. ~ NEDDY
A63. Gas with the knocks left in. ~ TIRADE

D1. The last thing a hungry man wants for a meal. ~ PIE
D2. A bone to pick. Take your choice, back bone, soup bone, wish bone or trombone. ~ OS
D3. A convertible seat in the new cars. ~ LAP
D4. A narrow "wet." ~ INLET
D5. Give it the once over. ~ SCAN
D6. The only time these stop their brooding is when they are stewed. ~ HENS
D7. You should walk right through this unless you have arch trouble. (plur.) ~ ARCADES
D8. An absorbing subject. ~ MERGER
D9. This was a terrible blow for those listening in. ~ BLARE
D10. These ducks will migrate from Washington, D. C., very soon. ~ LAME
D11. A teacher of wide renown. ~ EXPERIENCE
D12. The only cure for this is a home-run. ~ NOSTALGIA
D13. As mentioned above. ~ UPON
D22. The kind of joke that is rather dull. ~ TAME
D23. Whatever you think do not say it is this. ~ ME
D25. This one upholds wet hangouts. ~ CLOTHESPIN
D27. This is about the only thing that has paid during the past year. ~ PA
D28. What adversity makes us. ~ WISER
D29. A drop in the bucket. ~ ANTE
D30. Here is where you start on a long drive. ~ TEE
D31. These are deceitful. They promise more than they give. ~ PLEASURES
D33. Exists for you. ~ ARE
D34. This is all set for a June wedding. ~ WAVE
D35. What one is apt to do when sprig feber cubs on. ~ AIL
D37. The forecast seems to be for this kind of season. ~ WET
D38. This will never be yours. ~ MINE
D40. A rip tearing scorcher. ~ LAUNDRY
D41. The poor Chinaman. ~ LO
D43. Acted queerly because of the moonshine. ~ MOONED
D44. Another measure adopted to start employment for printers. ~ EM
D46. This is never still. ~ SOUND
D47. A star brand ham. ~ ACTOR
D49. Balls easily detected by golfers. ~ MOTH
D51. They named their horse this because it was bread in Kentucky. ~ PONE
D52. A word that any woman will get. ~ LAST
D53. This is up against it. ~ ANTI
D57. This is typically Scotch. ~ SMA
D58. One of the leading clubs in Bridgeport. ~ ACE
D61. This was sent out to sell and the first thring it did was to hit someone in the eye. ~ AD


